{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b11729ac-0000-4000-8000-b11729ac0001",
    "name": "Blitzy security evidence",
    "description": "Requests that produce the Postman screenshots for the security report.\n\nRun the folders in order. Folder 1 signs in and captures the ids the later requests need, so nothing has to be pasted by hand.\n\nEvery request has a test that asserts the status the report claims, so a green tick means the screenshot is the right one.\n\nBefore you start:\n  * Settings, General, Automatically follow redirects: OFF\n  * pick the `blitzy local` environment\n  * the API must be on http://localhost:8000 and the client on http://localhost:5173",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "event": [
    {
      "listen": "prerequest",
      "script": {
        "type": "text/javascript",
        "exec": [
          "// Origin first. Better Auth refuses any state-changing request without one",
          "// (MISSING_OR_NULL_ORIGIN), and a collection-level header is not always applied, so it is set",
          "// here where nothing can drop it.",
          "pm.request.headers.upsert({ key: 'Origin', value: pm.environment.get('origin') || 'http://localhost:5173' });",
          "",
          "// Requests whose name says the token is missing are the demonstration of the control, so they",
          "// must go out without one. This is decided by name rather than by a variable, because a",
          "// request-level script runs AFTER this one and would set the flag too late to matter.",
          "const wantsNoToken = /no CSRF token/i.test(pm.info.requestName || '');",
          "if (wantsNoToken) {",
          "  pm.request.headers.remove('x-csrf-token');",
          "  return;",
          "}",
          "",
          "// Everything else that changes state needs a token bound to the current session. Fetching it",
          "// here rather than pasting it by hand avoids the most common cause of a confusing 403.",
          "if (pm.request.method !== 'GET' && pm.request.url.getPath().indexOf('csrf-token') === -1) {",
          "  const base = pm.environment.get('base');",
          "  pm.sendRequest({",
          "    url: base + '/csrf-token',",
          "    method: 'GET',",
          "    header: { 'Origin': pm.environment.get('origin') || 'http://localhost:5173' }",
          "  }, function (err, res) {",
          "    if (!err && res.code === 200) {",
          "      const t = res.json().csrfToken;",
          "      pm.environment.set('csrf', t);",
          "      pm.request.headers.upsert({ key: 'x-csrf-token', value: t });",
          "    }",
          "  });",
          "}"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
  "header": [
    {
      "key": "Origin",
      "value": "{{origin}}"
    }
  ],
  "item": [
    {
      "name": "1 Setup, run this folder first",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Get a CSRF token",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/csrf-token",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "csrf-token"
              ]
            },
            "description": "The token endpoint. Never cached, so the value is fresh on every call."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('200', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));",
                  "pm.environment.set('csrf', pm.response.json().csrfToken);"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Sign in as sunita, the victim",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"email\": \"sunita@shop.test\",\n  \"password\": \"Nepal@Shop2026!\"\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/auth/sign-in/email",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "auth",
                "sign-in",
                "email"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 1. Open the Headers tab of the response and expand Set-Cookie.\n\nWhat to show: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite and the __Host- prefix.\n\nWhy it matters: HttpOnly is why a successful script injection still cannot read the session. The __Host- prefix means no subdomain can set a cookie this application would accept."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('signed in', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));",
                  "const c = pm.response.headers.get('set-cookie') || '';",
                  "pm.test('cookie is HttpOnly', () => pm.expect(c.toLowerCase()).to.include('httponly'));",
                  "pm.test('cookie is SameSite', () => pm.expect(c.toLowerCase()).to.include('samesite'));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Capture one of sunita's order ids",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/orders",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "orders"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Stores the first order id so the cross-customer test in folder 3 needs no manual paste."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('200', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));",
                  "const b = pm.response.json();",
                  "const list = b.orders || b.data || [];",
                  "if (list.length) {",
                  "  pm.environment.set('victimOrderId', list[0]._id || list[0].id);",
                  "  console.log('victimOrderId =', pm.environment.get('victimOrderId'));",
                  "} else {",
                  "  console.log('sunita has no orders. Place one in the app, or run: cd backend && npm run seed:all');",
                  "}"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Sign out",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/auth/sign-out",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "auth",
                "sign-out"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Ends the session so folder 3 can sign in as the attacker."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('signed out', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "2 Evidence, the screenshots",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Security headers on every response",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/products",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "products"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 2. Open the Headers tab.\n\nWhat to show: content-security-policy, strict-transport-security, x-content-type-options, referrer-policy.\n\nWhy it matters: these are applied centrally, so no route can forget them."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('200', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));",
                  "['content-security-policy','strict-transport-security','x-content-type-options','referrer-policy']",
                  "  .forEach(h => pm.test(h + ' present', () => pm.expect(pm.response.headers.has(h)).to.be.true));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "A write with no CSRF token is refused",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"items\": []\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/cart",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "cart"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 3. Expect 403.\n\nThe pre-request script recognises this request by its name and deliberately sends it without a token, so the control can be seen refusing it.\n\nWhat to show: the 403 and the body, which is an error string and a correlationId and nothing else. No stack trace, no table name, no library version.\n\nWhy it matters: two controls in one screenshot. The write is refused, and the failure discloses nothing."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('403 without a CSRF token', () => pm.response.to.have.status(403));",
                  "pm.test('body carries a correlation id', () => pm.expect(pm.response.text()).to.include('correlationId'));",
                  "pm.test('body leaks no stack trace', () => pm.expect(pm.response.text()).to.not.include('at '));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "The same write with a token succeeds",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"items\": []\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/cart",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "cart"
              ]
            },
            "description": "The control half of the pair above. Same request, same body, one header different."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('200 with a valid token', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "3 Attacks that are refused",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Sign in as bibek, the attacker",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"email\": \"bibek@shop.test\",\n  \"password\": \"Nepal@Shop2026!\"\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/auth/sign-in/email",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "auth",
                "sign-in",
                "email"
              ]
            }
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('signed in as the attacker', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "One customer reading another customer's order",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/orders/{{victimOrderId}}",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "orders",
                "{{victimOrderId}}"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 4. Expect 404, not 403.\n\nWhy it matters: a 403 would confirm the order exists and that permission is the only thing missing, which is exactly what an attacker needs to enumerate. A 404 reveals nothing.\n\nTwo independent controls produce this: the default-deny policy, and a row policy inside PostgreSQL that returns no rows even if the query forgot its filter."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('404, not 403', () => pm.response.to.have.status(404));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "An admin route as a normal customer",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/admin/users",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "admin",
                "users"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Expect 404. Admin rights are checked on the request, not in the interface."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('404', () => pm.response.to.have.status(404));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "SQL injection in the sort parameter",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/products?sort=(SELECT CASE WHEN (1=1) THEN name END)",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "products"
              ],
              "query": [
                {
                  "key": "sort",
                  "value": "(SELECT CASE WHEN (1=1) THEN name END)"
                }
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 5. Expect 400.\n\nWhy it matters: two layers, and this shows the first. The query schema accepts sort only as one of six fixed words, so the payload never reaches the database layer. If it did, the second layer would catch it: the key looks up a column object in a fixed map and is never concatenated into SQL.\n\nCompare with the next request, which is the valid form."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('400, the schema refuses it', () => pm.response.to.have.status(400));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "The valid sort, for contrast",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/products?sort=price-low",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "products"
              ],
              "query": [
                {
                  "key": "sort",
                  "value": "price-low"
                }
              ]
            },
            "description": "200 with the products cheapest first. Screenshot this beside the one above so the contrast is visible."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('200', () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Server-side request forgery to cloud metadata",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"url\": \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\"\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/uploads/products/image-from-url",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "uploads",
                "products",
                "image-from-url"
              ]
            },
            "description": "Expect 400 or 404.\n\nThe address is classified after DNS resolution rather than by reading the text of the URL, and the connection is pinned to the address that was checked, so a name that resolves differently the second time cannot slip through."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('refused', () => pm.expect([400, 403, 404]).to.include(pm.response.code));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "A malformed body, for the error handler",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{ this is not json"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/reviews",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "reviews"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 6. Expect 400 with a short message and a correlation id.\n\nWhy it matters: the id is the thread that links what the user saw to the full detail in the logs. The user gets nothing useful to an attacker, and an operator gets everything."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test('400', () => pm.response.to.have.status(400));",
                  "pm.test('no stack trace', () => pm.expect(pm.response.text()).to.not.include('.ts:'));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "4 Rate limiting, run this folder with the Runner",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Wrong password, run 11 iterations",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [
              {
                "key": "Content-Type",
                "value": "application/json"
              },
              {
                "key": "Origin",
                "value": "{{origin}}"
              }
            ],
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"email\": \"sunita@shop.test\",\n  \"password\": \"definitely-not-the-password\"\n}"
            },
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{base}}/auth/sign-in/email",
              "host": [
                "{{base}}"
              ],
              "path": [
                "auth",
                "sign-in",
                "email"
              ]
            },
            "description": "SCREENSHOT 7. Open the Runner, select only this folder, set iterations to 11, delay 0.\n\nWhat to show: the run summary. The first ten are 401 and the eleventh is 429.\n\nWhy it matters: the limit counts per account as well as per address, so rotating through addresses does not defeat it. Every failure is identical in body and status, so an attacker also cannot learn which accounts exist.\n\nThis also drives the Wazuh brute-force alert, rule 100010, and the auth_login_failure_total counter in Prometheus."
          },
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "const n = (pm.environment.get('attempt') || 0) * 1 + 1;",
                  "pm.environment.set('attempt', n);",
                  "pm.test('attempt ' + n + ' refused (' + pm.response.code + ')', function () {",
                  "  pm.expect([401, 429]).to.include(pm.response.code);",
                  "});",
                  "if (pm.response.code === 429) {",
                  "  pm.test('the limiter engaged at attempt ' + n, function () { pm.expect(true).to.be.true; });",
                  "}"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
